## Introduction 

As we discussed in DA3, GitHub is a platform that facilitates collaboration on coding and data science projects. In fact, for your final project, you are expected to create a GitHub repository that contains all your reproducible code and proper documentation that explains the structure and steps of the project. Following good practices from the beginning can be helpful since they:

- Help keep your code organized and clear. It makes it easy to find where each step in the analysis is performed.
- It makes it easier for others to read and reproduce your code.
- It is easier to work collaboratively, since it helps divide and organize tasks. 

In the present DA4, we explore some good practices that can help with well-maintaining your code and repository. 



## Step 0: Naming 

Naming is vital to keeping a repository organized. One useful approach is to number files so that they appear in a logical order. Complete the following step.

0a) Rename this file to `11_nbaAnalysis_dataProcessing.Rmd`. You will see the usefulness of this numbering convention as we move forward. 



## Step 1: Creating a `00_requirements.R` File

When running a data analysis in R, it is often useful to know what packages you need for the analysis. One easy way to do this is by creating an __R package setup script__ that installs and loads all packages required for your analysis. With this in mind, complete the following steps.

1a) In the folder that you created to run DA4, create a plain R-code file and name it `00_requirements.R` 

1b) Use the example code shared and expand it to install and include the packages `tidyverse`, `readxl` and `magrittr`.

```{r}

if (!require(tidyverse)) {       ## If package is not installed  
  install.packages("tidyverse") ## Install
  library(tidyverse)            ## Load package to environment.
}

```


1c) Create a code chunk below this line, and include the line `source("00_requirements.R")`. Print the output of the function `search()` to verify that you successfully installed and included the package in the current environment. We will study more on packages later. 


For your project, ensure that you create this `00_requirements.R` file. Every time you need a package, include it in your `00_requirements.R` file. Then, load the packages in each R Markdown file with the `source()` function.

```{r}
source("00_requirements.R")
print(search())

```




