## Step 3: Saving environments

Because data analyses can be complex, it is often best to process data in stages. Often one has to perform cleaning, processing, visualizing, modeling and generating final outcomes of the data analysis separately. Usually, at each step data are modified, and keeping track of how the dataset changes throughout the process is essential. 



A good way to keep track of how the data changes at each stage is by saving **R work environments.** This means that you can save the current stage of the R environment, to load later. To practice this, complete the following exercise.


3a) Download the dataset `00_regression_nba.xlsx` to your `DA4` folder. Then, on a chunk of code below, import this dataset into R. (Hint: consider installing and using the package `readxl`)

3b) Create a chunk and rename the variable `"player_height (cm)"` to `height_in` and `"player_weight (kg)"` to `weight_lbs`. Then, convert these columns to the new units. Use the command `rm()` to remove the objects `colmean` and `x_matrix` from the environment. 

3c) Create a chunk and add the command `save.image("12_nbaAnalysisData.RData")`. This will save the environment. Then, add the command `rm(list = ls())`, which empties the environment. Finally, print the outcome of `ls()` to verify that the environment is now empty.

3d) Below these instructions, create a code chunk and add the command `load("12_nbaAnalysisData.RData")`. Print the outcome of `ls()` to verify that you restored the variables that used to be in your environment. Then, display the first six rows of the processed dataset, Verify that the names are changed as desired, and they are in the appropriate new units. 



